  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This eLife article offers a mechanistic hypothesis for how <i>Fusobacterium nucleatum</i> may influence antitumor immunity through the NK cell receptor NKp46. eLife judged the study useful but noted that the mechanistic evidence is incomplete, so the record supports interest and early uptake more strongly than settled conclusion. The article is open access, has documented reuse in Europe PMC, and already appears in one 2026 citing article in Journal of Periodontal Research.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published in eLife in 2025 by Ahmed Rishiq, Johanna Galski, Reem Bsoul, Mingdong Liu, Rema Darawshe, Renate Lux, Gilad Bachrach, and Ofer Mandelboim, this study focuses on a counterintuitive aspect of <i>Fusobacterium nucleatum</i>: a bacterium best known for associations with disease may also shape antitumor responses. The authors report a subspecies-specific interaction between the bacterial adhesin RadD and NKp46, positioning the work at the intersection of microbiology, cancer immunology, and cytotoxicity.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest evidence of early influence is scholarly rather than public. eLife\u2019s assessment describes the study as useful, but also explicitly characterizes the evidentiary support for the central mechanistic claim as incomplete. That matters for how the work should be read: it has clearly attracted attention for a provocative immunological mechanism, yet the available record advises caution about treating the RadD\u2013NKp46 interaction as fully settled.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">In terms of uptake, the article has one documented downstream citation in a 2026 review-like article in <i>Journal of Periodontal Research</i>, which situates the paper within ongoing discussion of the role of <i>F. nucleatum</i> in cancer. The reuse graph also records one Europe PMC article mention, indicating that the paper is already being incorporated into the biomedical literature ecosystem even while citation volume remains very early.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The publication is supported by multiple Israeli funding sources, including the Israeli Innovation Authority and the Israel Science Foundation, and its open-access status should make the findings easy to follow up by other researchers. Overall, the record shows a promising but still provisional contribution: a mechanistically focused cancer-immunology paper with clear conceptual interest, early scholarly reuse, and an editorial assessment that highlights the need for stronger evidence before the proposed pathway can be considered definitive.</p>",
